e\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n3478,STATISTICAL APPROACH,The statistical approach to designing experiments involves various methodologies and techniques for conducting experiments and analyzing data.,6\r\n603,REGRESSION MODELS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Regression Models**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nRegression models are statistical processes designed to estimate the relationships among variables, primarily focusing on predicting the value of a dependent variable based on one or more independent variables. These models serve as essential tools in data analysis and predictive modeling, allowing researchers and analysts to understand how changes in predictor variables influence the outcome variable.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe general mathematical formulation of a regression model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + ... + \\beta_nX_n + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( Y \\) is the dependent variable,\n- \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept,\n- \\( \\beta_1, \\beta_2, ..., \\beta_n \\) are the coefficients of the independent variables \\( X_1, X_2, ..., X_n \\),\n- \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nRegression models can be structured in various forms, including:\n- **Simple Linear Regression:** Involves one independent variable and one dependent variable.\n- **Multiple Linear Regression:** Involves two or more independent variables.\n- **Polynomial Regression:** Involves independent variables raised to a power.\n- **Logistic Regression:** Used for binary dependent variables.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nTo ensure the validity of regression models, several key assumptions must be met:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables is linear.\n2. Independence: Observations are independent of each other.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of the residuals is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. Normality: The residuals of the model are normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nRegression models are applicable when the goal is to understand the relationship between variables and to make predictions. They are particularly useful when:\n- The dependent variable is continuous.\n- The researcher seeks to quantify the effect of one or more predictors.\n- The assumptions of linearity and normality are reasonably satisfied.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure for regression models typically involves:\n1. Specifying the model structure.\n2. Estimating the coefficients using methods such as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS).\n3. Evaluating the model fit using metrics like R-squared, Adjusted R-squared, and residual analysis.\n4. Conducting hypothesis tests on the coefficients to determine their significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Regression models provide a clear framework for understanding relationships between variables.\n- They allow for the quantification of the strength and direction of these relationships.\n- The models can be easily extended to include interaction terms and polynomial terms for more complex relationships.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Regression models can be sensitive to outliers, which may skew results.\n- They rely heavily on the assumptions mentioned earlier; violations can lead to misleading conclusions.\n- Overfitting can occur when too many predictors are included, reducing the model's generalizability.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methods include:\n- Generalized Linear Models (GLMs) for non-normal response variables.\n- Time Series Analysis for data collected over time.\n- Machine Learning techniques such as decision trees and neural networks for more complex relationships.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing regression models, practitioners should consider:\n- The selection of appropriate independent variables based on theoretical understanding and exploratory data analysis.\n- The need for data preprocessing, including handling missing values and scaling variables.\n- The importance of validating the model using techniques such as cross-validation.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nRegression models can be implemented using various statistical software packages, including R, Python (with libraries such as scikit-learn and statsmodels), SAS, and SPSS. These tools provide built-in functions for model fitting, diagnostics, and visualization.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nRegression models are widely used across various fields, including:\n- Economics for predicting consumer spending.\n- Medicine for assessing the impact of treatments on health outcomes.\n- Marketing for analyzing the effectiveness of advertising campaigns.\n- Environmental science for modeling the relationship between pollutants and health effects. \n\nIn summary, regression models are versatile statistical tools that facilitate the understanding and prediction of relationships among variables, provided that their assumptions are adequately addressed and the models are appropriately specified.\",7\r\n604,RESPONSE SURFACE METHODOLOGY,\"**Response Surface Methodology (RSM)**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nResponse Surface Methodology (RSM) is a collection of statistical and mathematical techniques designed to model and analyze problems where a response of interest is influenced by multiple variables. The primary purpose of RSM is to optimize processes by exploring the relationships between several explanatory variables and one or more response variables. It is particularly useful in the context of experimental design, where the goal is to improve, develop, or optimize processes and product designs.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nRSM typically involves fitting a polynomial model to the response surface. The general form of the second-order polynomial model can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\epsilon \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y \\) is the response variable,\n- \\( X_i \\) are the independent variables (factors),\n- \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept,\n- \\( \\beta_i \\) are the linear coefficients,\n- \\( \\beta_{ij} \\) are the interaction coefficients,\n- \\( k \\) is the number of factors,\n- \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nRSM often employs a central composite design (CCD) or Box-Behnken design to efficiently explore the response surface. These designs allow for the estimation of the coefficients in the polynomial model while minimizing the number of experimental runs required.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of RSM relies on several assumptions:\n1. The relationship between the response and the factors is approximately linear in the region of interest.\n2. The errors are normally distributed with constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The observations are independent.\n4. The model is correctly specified, meaning that the true relationship can be adequately captured by the chosen polynomial form.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nRSM is applicable when:\n- The objective is to optimize a response influenced by multiple factors.\n- The relationship between factors and the response is not well understood.\n- There is a need to explore interactions between factors.\n- The experimental region is continuous and can be modeled with polynomial functions.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis typically involves:\n1. Designing the experiment using a suitable RSM design (e.g., CCD or Box-Behnken).\n2. Conducting the experiments and collecting data.\n3. Fitting the polynomial model to the data using regression analysis.\n4. Analyzing the fitted model to identify significant factors and interactions.\n5. Using optimization techniques to find the optimal settings of the factors.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- RSM provides a systematic approach to optimization, allowing for the exploration of interactions between factors.\n- It can reduce the number of experimental runs needed compared to full factorial designs.\n- The methodology is flexible and can be adapted to various types of responses and experimental conditions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- RSM is primarily effective in a limited region of the factor space; extrapolation beyond this region can lead to inaccurate predictions.\n- The assumption of a polynomial relationship may not hold true for all processes.\n- It may require a significant amount of data to accurately estimate the model parameters, especially in higher dimensions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRSM is related to other experimental design techniques such as factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and Taguchi methods. Alternatives to RSM include machine learning approaches for optimization, which may not rely on the same assumptions about the response surface.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing RSM, practitioners should consider the selection of appropriate factor levels, the need for replication to assess variability, and the potential for confounding effects. It is also important to ensure that the experimental conditions are controlled to minimize noise.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nRSM can be implemented using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, Minitab, and JMP, which provide tools for designing experiments, fitting models, and conducting optimization analyses.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nRSM is widely used in various fields, including manufacturing, pharmaceuticals, agriculture, and food science. Common applications include optimizing chemical processes, improving product formulations, and enhancing quality control measures.\",19\r\n2327,OPTIMAL DESIGNS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Optimal Designs**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nOptimal Designs for Response Surfaces are specialized experimental designs aimed at maximizing the efficiency of parameter estimation for response surface models. These designs are particularly useful in scenarios where standard designs, such as factorial or fractional factorial designs, may not be adequate due to irregular experimental regions or the presence of nonstandard models. The primary purpose of optimal designs is to achieve the best statistical properties for estimating model parameters, thereby enhancing the reliability and accuracy of the conclusions drawn from the experimental data.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nOptimal designs are typically formulated based on criteria that quantify the efficiency of the design, such as D-optimality, A-optimality, or E-optimality. For instance, a D-optimal design seeks to maximize the determinant of the information matrix, which is related to the precision of the estimated parameters. The general structure of an optimal design can be represented mathematically as:\n\n\\[ \\text{maximize} \\, |X'X| \\]\n\nwhere \\(X\\) is the design matrix containing the levels of the factors in the experiment.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nThe layout of optimal designs can vary significantly depending on the specific criteria used and the nature of the response surface being investigated. These designs may involve a combination of factor levels that are strategically chosen to cover the experimental space efficiently, often leading to non-regular arrangements that differ from traditional designs.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nFor optimal designs to be valid, several assumptions must be met:\n1. The response surface is adequately modeled by a polynomial function.\n2. The errors associated with the observations are normally distributed and independent.\n3. The variance of the errors is constant across the experimental region (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nOptimal designs are particularly applicable in situations where:\n- The experimental region is irregular or complex.\n- The underlying model is nonstandard or not well-represented by traditional designs.\n- There is a need for high precision in parameter estimation with limited resources.\n\nSelection criteria often include the specific goals of the experiment, the nature of the response surface, and the computational resources available for design generation.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of data obtained from optimal designs typically involves fitting the chosen response surface model to the data using regression techniques. The efficiency of the design can be assessed through the evaluation of the estimated parameters' variances and the overall model fit.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe strengths of optimal designs include:\n- Enhanced efficiency in parameter estimation, leading to more reliable results.\n- Flexibility in accommodating complex experimental regions and nonstandard models.\n- The ability to provide designs that are tailored to specific experimental objectives.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nDespite their advantages, optimal designs also have limitations:\n- They can be computationally intensive to generate, especially for high-dimensional problems.\n- The complexity of the design may make it less intuitive for practitioners unfamiliar with advanced design concepts.\n- Optimal designs may not always be robust to model misspecification.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated designs include traditional factorial designs, response surface methodology (RSM), and Bayesian designs. Alternatives may involve using standard designs with additional runs or augmentations to improve efficiency.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing optimal designs, practitioners should consider the computational tools available for design generation and analysis, as well as the specific context of the experiment. It is crucial to ensure that the assumptions underlying the design are met and to validate the model post-experimentation.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nSeveral statistical software packages, such as R (with packages like `AlgDesign` and `rsm`), SAS, and MATLAB, provide tools for generating optimal designs and conducting subsequent analyses.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nOptimal designs are commonly used in fields such as chemical engineering, pharmaceuticals, and agricultural research, where precise modeling of complex systems is essential. Examples include optimizing reaction conditions in chemical processes or determining the best formulation in product development.\",4\r\n2326,BLOCKING IN RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGNS,Blocking in Response Surface Designs is a technique used to reduce variability in experiments by grouping similar experimental units.,2\r\n2161,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,\"**Response Surface Design: Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nResponse Surface Design (RSD) is a statistical technique employed to model and analyze the relationship between multiple explanatory variables (factors) and one or more response variables (outputs). The primary purpose of RSD is to optimize processes by exploring the effects of the factors on the response, identifying optimal conditions, and understanding the interactions between variables.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe general mathematical model for a response surface can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = f(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k) + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( f \\) is a function of the explanatory variables \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the random error. The function \\( f \\) is typically approximated using polynomial regression, often a second-order polynomial:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j \\]\nwhere \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the linear coefficients, and \\( \\beta_{ij} \\) are the interaction coefficients.\n\n**Design Layout:**\nResponse surface designs can be structured in various ways, including Central Composite Designs (CCD), Box-Behnken Designs, and Full Factorial Designs. These designs allow for the systematic exploration of the response surface by varying the levels of the factors.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\n1. The relationship between the factors and the response is approximately linear or can be adequately modeled by a polynomial.\n2. The errors are normally distributed with constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The observations are independent.\n4. The experimental region is adequately explored, and the design points are well-distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nResponse surface designs are particularly useful when:\n- The researcher seeks to optimize a response variable influenced by multiple factors.\n- There is a need to understand the interaction effects between factors.\n- The experimental region is not well-defined, and exploratory analysis is required.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves fitting a regression model to the collected data, assessing the significance of the factors and their interactions, and using diagnostic plots to validate model assumptions. Techniques such as Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) are employed to evaluate the model fit and identify significant predictors.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- RSD allows for the exploration of complex relationships between multiple factors and responses.\n- It provides a systematic approach to optimization, reducing the number of experiments needed compared to full factorial designs.\n- The methodology is flexible and can be adapted to various experimental contexts.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The requirement for a well-defined experimental region can limit applicability.\n- The assumption of a polynomial relationship may not hold true in all cases, leading to model misspecification.\n- The complexity of the design and analysis may require advanced statistical knowledge.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nResponse surface designs are related to other experimental designs such as factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and Taguchi methods. Alternatives include optimization techniques like genetic algorithms and simulated annealing, which do not rely on a predefined response surface.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing RSD, researchers should consider the selection of appropriate factor levels, the number of replications for estimating experimental error, and the need for randomization to mitigate bias. It is also essential to ensure that the experimental conditions are controlled to minimize variability.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nVarious statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide tools for designing response surface experiments, fitting models, and conducting ANOVA. These tools facilitate the analysis and visualization of response surfaces.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nResponse surface designs are widely used in fields such as engineering, pharmaceuticals, agriculture, and food science. Common applications include optimizing chemical reactions, improving product formulations, and enhancing manufacturing processes.\",12\r\n155,PARAMETERS,\"**Summary of Parameters in Statistical Models and Experimental Designs**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nParameters are numerical characteristics that define the behavior and structure of statistical models and experimental designs. They serve as essential components that influence outcomes and interpretations in various statistical analyses, including regression models and block designs. Parameters can include values such as means, variances, coefficients, and structural elements of designs.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn statistical models, parameters often take the form of coefficients in regression equations, which can be represented as:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + ... + \\beta_nX_n + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the dependent variable, \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the parameters (coefficients) for independent variables \\( X_i \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term. In the context of experimental designs, parameters define the structure, such as the number of treatments, blocks, and replications.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn the context of experimental designs, parameters specify the layout of the design. For instance, in a Randomized Block Design (RBD), parameters include the number of blocks and treatments. In a Partially Balanced Incomplete Block Design (PBIBD), parameters define the number of treatments, blocks, and replicates, which dictate how treatments are allocated across blocks.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of parameters in statistical models and designs relies on several assumptions, including:\n- Independence of observations\n- Normality of residuals\n- Homogeneity of variances (homoscedasticity)\n- Correct specification of the model\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nParameters are applicable in various statistical analyses, including regression analysis, ANOVA, and experimental design. The selection of parameters depends on the research question, the nature of the data, and the specific design requirements. For example, in block designs, the number of treatments and blocks must be carefully considered to ensure adequate power and control of variability.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis involving parameters typically includes estimating the parameters from the data using methods such as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) for regression models or ANOVA techniques for experimental designs. The estimated parameters are then used to make inferences about the population or to predict outcomes.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe use of parameters allows for a structured approach to modeling and analyzing data. They provide a clear framework for understanding relationships between variables and for designing experiments that can yield reliable and valid results. Parameters also facilitate the quantification of uncertainty and variability in statistical analyses.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nOne limitation of parameters is that they can be sensitive to model specification. Incorrectly specified models can lead to biased parameter estimates. Additionally, the complexity of models can increase with the number of parameters, potentially leading to overfitting, especially in small sample sizes.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nParameters are integral to various designs and methods, including Completely Randomized Designs (CRD), Factorial Designs, and Response Surface Methodology (RSM). Each of these approaches has its own set of parameters that define their structure and analysis.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing parameters in statistical analyses, researchers must ensure that the chosen parameters align with the experimental design and the underlying assumptions of the statistical methods used. Careful planning and consideration of sample size, treatment allocation, and replication are crucial for obtaining valid results.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nParameters can be estimated and analyzed using various statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python libraries. These tools provide functions for fitting models, conducting ANOVA, and performing regression analyses, allowing researchers to efficiently work with parameters in their data.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nParameters are widely used in fields such as agriculture, medicine, and social sciences. For example, in agricultural experiments, parameters may define the number of different fertilizer treatments applied to crops, while in clinical trials, parameters may represent the dosage levels of a drug being tested. In both cases, the careful selection and estimation of parameters are critical for drawing valid conclusions from the data.\",8\r\n2333,\"MYERS, MONTGOMERY AND ANDERSON-COOK\",\"**MYERS, MONTGOMERY AND ANDERSON-COOK: A Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nMyers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook are prominent authors recognized for their significant contributions to the fields of experimental design and response surface methodology (RSM). Their work primarily focuses on the development and application of statistical techniques that enhance process robustness and optimize responses in various experimental settings.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nWhile specific mathematical formulations are not detailed in the descriptions, response surface methodology typically involves fitting a polynomial model to the response variable as a function of the input factors. The general form of a second-order polynomial model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( X_i \\) are the input factors, \\( \\beta \\) are the coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nThe design structures advocated by Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook include various types of response surface designs, such as central composite designs (CCD) and Box-Behnken designs. These designs are structured to efficiently explore the relationships between multiple factors and their effects on the response variable.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of the methodologies proposed by Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook relies on several assumptions, including:\n- The relationship between the factors and the response is approximately linear or can be adequately modeled by a polynomial.\n- The errors are normally distributed and independent.\n- Homoscedasticity, meaning constant variance of errors across all levels of the factors.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nResponse surface methodology is applicable when the goal is to optimize a response influenced by several variables. It is particularly useful in situations where:\n- The relationship between factors and responses is not well understood.\n- There is a need to identify optimal conditions for a process.\n- The number of factors is moderate, allowing for comprehensive exploration without excessive experimentation.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves fitting a regression model to the collected data, followed by diagnostic checks for model adequacy. Techniques such as ANOVA are employed to assess the significance of the factors and their interactions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe methodologies developed by Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook offer several advantages:\n- They provide a systematic approach to optimizing processes.\n- The designs are efficient, often requiring fewer experimental runs than full factorial designs.\n- They facilitate the exploration of interactions between factors.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nDespite their strengths, there are limitations:\n- The need for a reasonable approximation of the response surface; if the true relationship is highly nonlinear, polynomial models may not suffice.\n- The complexity of the analysis increases with the number of factors and interactions considered.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methodologies include factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and Taguchi methods for robust parameter design. Each of these approaches has its own strengths and is chosen based on specific experimental goals.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing the methodologies, practitioners should consider the following:\n- The selection of appropriate factor levels and ranges.\n- The need for replication to ensure reliability of results.\n- The importance of randomization to mitigate bias.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nWhile specific software implementations are not mentioned in the descriptions, popular statistical software packages such as R, SAS, and Minitab provide tools for conducting response surface methodology and analyzing experimental designs.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nCommon applications of the methodologies developed by Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook include:\n- Optimization of manufacturing processes.\n- Development of new products in various industries, including pharmaceuticals and food production.\n- Studies aimed at improving quality and performance in engineering and scientific research.\n\nIn summary, Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook have made substantial contributions to the fields of experimental design and response surface methodology, providing valuable frameworks and techniques for optimizing processes and enhancing robustness in various applications.\",5\r\n2332,KHURI AND CORNELL,\"**Summary of Khuri and Cornell in Response Surface Methodology**\n\nKhuri and Cornell are prominent authors recognized for their significant contributions to the field of Response Surface Methodology (RSM), particularly through their comprehensive presentation published in 1996. Their work serves as a foundational reference for understanding and applying response surface designs in experimental statistics.\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nResponse Surface Methodology is a collection of mathematical and statistical techniques used for modeling and analyzing problems in which a response of interest is influenced by several variables. The primary purpose of RSM is to optimize this response by exploring the relationships between the response and the independent variables, often through the use of polynomial regression models.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nThe typical model structure in RSM involves fitting a polynomial equation to the response variable \\( Y \\) as a function of the independent variables \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\). A common form of the second-order polynomial model is given by:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\epsilon\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the linear coefficients, \\( \\beta_{ij} \\) are the interaction coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nRSM typically employs a central composite design (CCD) or Box-Behnken design to efficiently explore the response surface. These designs allow for the estimation of the coefficients in the polynomial model while minimizing the number of experimental runs required.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of RSM relies on several assumptions:\n1. The relationship between the response and the independent variables is adequately captured by the polynomial model.\n2. The errors are normally distributed with constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The observations are independent of one another.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nRSM is applicable when:\n- The goal is to optimize a response variable influenced by multiple factors.\n- The relationship between the factors and the response is expected to be nonlinear.\n- Preliminary experiments have been conducted to identify significant factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of RSM typically involves:\n1. Fitting the polynomial model to the experimental data using regression analysis.\n2. Conducting hypothesis tests to evaluate the significance of the model terms.\n3. Performing diagnostic checks to validate model assumptions.\n4. Using response surface plots to visualize the relationships and identify optimal conditions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe strengths of RSM include:\n- Efficient exploration of the response surface with fewer experimental runs compared to full factorial designs.\n- Ability to identify interaction effects and nonlinear relationships.\n- Provides a systematic approach to optimization.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nLimitations of RSM include:\n- The requirement for a well-defined experimental region, as extrapolation beyond the studied range can lead to unreliable predictions.\n- The polynomial model may not adequately capture complex relationships if the true response surface is highly nonlinear or has multiple peaks.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methodologies include factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and other optimization techniques such as Taguchi methods. Each of these approaches has its own strengths and is suitable for different experimental contexts.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing RSM, researchers should consider:\n- The selection of appropriate levels for each factor to ensure a comprehensive exploration of the response surface.\n- The need for replication to assess the variability in the response.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nVarious statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide tools for conducting RSM analyses, including design generation, model fitting, and visualization of response surfaces.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nRSM is widely used in various fields, including engineering, pharmaceuticals, and agriculture, for optimizing processes such as chemical reactions, product formulations, and manufacturing processes. Khuri and Cornell's work serves as a critical reference for practitioners seeking to apply RSM effectively in their experimental designs.\",4\r\n2334,BOX AND DRAPER,\"**Box and Draper: Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nBox and Draper are prominent authors in the field of statistics, particularly known for their contributions to Response Surface Methodology (RSM). Their work, notably encapsulated in a book published in 2007, serves as a foundational reference for understanding and applying response surface methods in experimental design. The primary purpose of their contributions is to provide a systematic approach to optimizing processes and products through the exploration of relationships between multiple factors and responses.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nWhile specific mathematical formulations are not detailed in the provided descriptions, Response Surface Methodology typically involves fitting a polynomial model to the response variable as a function of the independent variables (factors). The general form of a second-order polynomial model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response, \\( X_i \\) are the factors, \\( \\beta \\) are the coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nBox and Draper emphasize the importance of selecting appropriate experimental designs for RSM, which may include central composite designs (CCD), Box-Behnken designs, and factorial designs. These designs facilitate the estimation of the response surface and the identification of optimal conditions.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of the models and methods proposed by Box and Draper relies on several assumptions:\n1. The relationship between the factors and the response is approximately linear or can be adequately modeled by a polynomial.\n2. The errors are normally distributed and independent.\n3. Homoscedasticity, or constant variance of errors, is assumed across the range of the independent variables.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nBox and Draper’s methodologies are applicable in scenarios where the goal is to optimize a response influenced by multiple factors. Selection criteria for using RSM include:\n- The need for a systematic approach to explore the effects of several variables.\n- Situations where preliminary experiments suggest a non-linear relationship between factors and responses.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves fitting a regression model to the collected data, followed by diagnostic checks for model adequacy. Techniques such as ANOVA are employed to assess the significance of the factors and their interactions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe strengths of Box and Draper’s contributions include:\n- A structured approach to optimization that can handle multiple factors simultaneously.\n- The ability to identify interactions between factors, which is crucial for understanding complex systems.\n- Comprehensive guidance on experimental design choices and methodologies.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nLimitations include:\n- The requirement for a sufficient number of experimental runs, which can be resource-intensive.\n- The potential for overfitting if the model is too complex relative to the amount of data available.\n- The assumption of a polynomial relationship may not hold true in all cases.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methodologies include factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and Taguchi methods, which also focus on optimizing processes but may differ in their approach and complexity.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing Box and Draper’s methodologies, practitioners should consider the following:\n- The need for adequate sample sizes to ensure reliable estimates of the response surface.\n- The importance of randomization in experimental runs to mitigate bias.\n- The necessity of conducting follow-up experiments to validate findings.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nWhile specific software implementations are not mentioned in the descriptions, common statistical software packages such as R, SAS, and Minitab provide tools for conducting RSM and analyzing data using the methodologies proposed by Box and Draper.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nBox and Draper’s work is widely applicable in various fields, including manufacturing, agriculture, and pharmaceuticals, where optimizing processes and product formulations is critical. An example highlighted in their work is the Worsted Yarn Experiment, which illustrates the practical application of RSM in a real-world context.\",6\r\n2169,EXPERIMENTAL DESIGNS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Experimental Designs**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nExperimental designs are structured plans for conducting experiments that ensure the data collected can be analyzed effectively. They serve the purpose of investigating the effects of independent variables (factors) on dependent variables (responses), thereby facilitating the understanding of relationships between variables and optimizing outcomes.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nWhile specific mathematical formulations can vary depending on the type of experimental design employed (e.g., completely randomized design, factorial design, etc.), a general model structure can be represented as:  \n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]  \nwhere \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable, \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the effect of the \\( i \\)-th treatment or factor level, and \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the random error associated with the \\( j \\)-th observation under the \\( i \\)-th treatment.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nExperimental designs can take various forms, including but not limited to:\n- **Completely Randomized Design (CRD)**: Treatments are assigned randomly to experimental units.\n- **Randomized Block Design (RBD)**: Experimental units are grouped into blocks based on a nuisance variable, and treatments are randomly assigned within each block.\n- **Factorial Design**: All possible combinations of factors and their levels are tested, allowing for the examination of interaction effects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nKey assumptions for the validity of experimental designs include:\n- Independence of observations.\n- Normality of the response variable.\n- Homogeneity of variances (equal variances across groups).\n- Random assignment of treatments to experimental units.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nExperimental designs are applicable in various research contexts where the goal is to determine causal relationships between variables. Selection criteria include the nature of the research question, the number of factors and levels to be tested, resource availability, and the need for controlling variability.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of data collected from experimental designs typically involves statistical methods such as Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to assess the significance of the effects of factors on the response variable. Post-hoc tests may be conducted to explore differences between specific treatment means.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe strengths of experimental designs include:\n- Ability to control for confounding variables.\n- Facilitation of causal inference.\n- Structured approach that enhances the reliability and validity of results.\n- Flexibility in design choice to suit specific research needs.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nLimitations of experimental designs may include:\n- Potential for bias if randomization is not properly implemented.\n- Complexity in design and analysis, especially with multiple factors.\n- Ethical or practical constraints in manipulating certain variables.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methodologies include observational studies, quasi-experimental designs, and non-parametric methods. Each alternative has its own strengths and weaknesses depending on the research context.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing experimental designs, researchers should consider factors such as sample size determination, resource allocation, and the feasibility of randomization. Proper training in statistical methods and experimental protocols is also essential.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nVarious statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Minitab, provide tools for designing experiments and analyzing data. These tools often include built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and generating design matrices.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nExperimental designs are widely used in fields such as agriculture (e.g., testing crop yields under different fertilizer treatments), medicine (e.g., clinical trials for new drugs), and industrial processes (e.g., optimizing manufacturing conditions). They are essential for advancing knowledge and improving practices across diverse domains.\",10\r\n2398,VARIANCE DISPERSION GRAPH,The Variance Dispersion Graph (VDG) is a graphical representation used to display prediction variance for design and response models.,4\r\n2325,SECOND-ORDER RESPONSE SURFACE,The Second-Order Response Surface is a model that describes the relationship between the response variable and the factors in a quadratic form.,3\r\n2403,DESIGN POINT,,1\r\n2408,A-OPTIMALITY CRITERION,The A-optimality criterion minimizes the sum of the variances of the regression coefficients in an experimental design.,1\r\n1238,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE TABLE,\"### Analysis of Variance Table\n\nThe Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) Table is a crucial component in the statistical analysis of experimental data, specifically designed to summarize the results of an ANOVA. Its primary purpose is to elucidate the sources of variation within a dataset and quantify their contributions to the total variance observed in the experiment. By doing so, it facilitates the determination of the significance of different factors and their interactions, thereby aiding in the understanding of the underlying processes affecting the response variable.\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation and Model Structure\nThe ANOVA Table is structured around the partitioning of total variance into components attributable to different sources. Mathematically, the total sum of squares (SST) can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ SST = \\sum (Y_{ij} - \\bar{Y})^2 \\]\n\nwhere \\( Y_{ij} \\) represents the individual observations, and \\( \\bar{Y} \\) is the overall mean. The total sum of squares is divided into the sum of squares for treatments (SSTr) and the sum of squares for error (SSE):\n\n\\[ SST = SSTr + SSE \\]\n\nThe degrees of freedom (df) associated with each source of variation are also calculated, and the mean squares (MS) are derived by dividing the sum of squares by their respective degrees of freedom:\n\n\\[ MS = \\frac{SS}{df} \\]\n\nThe ANOVA Table typically includes columns for the source of variation, sum of squares, degrees of freedom, mean squares, and the F-statistic, which is used to test the null hypothesis that all group means are equal.\n\n#### Design Layout or Structure\nThe ANOVA Table is typically organized in a tabular format, with rows representing different sources of variation (e.g., treatments, error) and columns detailing the sum of squares, degrees of freedom, mean squares, and F-statistics. This structured layout allows for easy comparison of the contributions of each factor to the overall variability.\n\n#### Assumptions Required for Validity\nFor the results derived from the ANOVA Table to be valid, several key assumptions must be met:\n1. **Independence**: Observations must be independent of one another.\n2. **Normality**: The residuals (differences between observed and predicted values) should be normally distributed.\n3. **Homogeneity of Variance**: The variances among the groups being compared should be approximately equal.\n\n#### When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria\nThe ANOVA Table is applicable in situations where researchers seek to compare means across multiple groups or treatments. It is particularly useful when:\n- The experimental design involves more than two groups.\n- The researcher aims to understand the impact of categorical independent variables on a continuous dependent variable.\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nThe analysis procedure involves:\n1. Formulating the null and alternative hypotheses.\n2. Calculating the sum of squares for each source of variation.\n3. Determining the degrees of freedom and mean squares.\n4. Computing the F-statistic and comparing it against a critical value from the F-distribution to assess significance.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\nThe ANOVA Table provides several advantages:\n- It allows for the simultaneous comparison of multiple group means.\n- It helps identify significant factors and interactions in a systematic manner.\n- It is a foundational tool in experimental design and analysis, facilitating robust statistical inference.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\nDespite its strengths, the ANOVA Table has limitations:\n- It is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variance.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for that purpose.\n- It can be complex to interpret in the presence of interactions among factors.\n\n#### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nThe ANOVA Table is closely related to various designs and methods, including:\n- Factorial ANOVA, which examines multiple factors simultaneously.\n- Repeated measures ANOVA, used for correlated groups.\n- Non-parametric alternatives such as the Kruskal-Wallis test when assumptions are violated.\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing an ANOVA Table, researchers should ensure proper experimental design, including randomization and replication, to enhance the reliability of results. It is also essential to conduct diagnostic checks for the assumptions of ANOVA.\n\n#### Software Implementation\nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python's SciPy library, provide built-in functions to perform ANOVA and generate ANOVA Tables, making it accessible for researchers to conduct their analyses efficiently.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nThe ANOVA Table is widely used across various fields, including agriculture (to compare crop yields under different treatments), psychology (to assess the effects of different therapies), and manufacturing (to evaluate the impact of different production methods on product quality). It serves as a fundamental tool in experimental research, enabling researchers to draw meaningful conclusions from their data.\",3\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n4487,STATISTICAL APPROACH,ANOVA,The statistical approach often includes methods like ANOVA for analyzing variance among groups,169\r\n661,REGRESSION MODELS,RESPONSE SURFACE METHODOLOGY,Regression Models are often employed within Response Surface Methodology to model and analyze the relationships between factors and responses.,26\r\n3173,RESPONSE SURFACE METHODOLOGY,OPTIMAL DESIGNS,Optimal Designs are a critical aspect of Response Surface Methodology aimed at improving the efficiency of experiments,23\r\n662,RESPONSE SURFACE METHODOLOGY,ROBUST PARAMETER DESIGN,Response Surface Methodology is utilized in Robust Parameter Design to optimize processes and improve product quality.,22\r\n3172,RESPONSE SURFACE METHODOLOGY,BLOCKING IN RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGNS,Blocking is a technique used in Response Surface Methodology to control variability in experimental designs,21\r\n3310,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,CCD,Response Surface Design includes Central Composite Design (CCD) as a method for modeling,21\r\n3043,PARAMETERS,COVARIANCE MATRIX,The covariance matrix is used to assess the variability and relationships of the parameters in regression models,19\r\n3277,\"MYERS, MONTGOMERY AND ANDERSON-COOK\",RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,\"Myers, Montgomery, and Anderson-Cook contributed significantly to the understanding and application of response surface designs\",17\r\n3275,KHURI AND CORNELL,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,\"Khuri and Cornell's work is foundational in the field of response surface design, providing methodologies and insights\",16\r\n3279,BOX AND DRAPER,EXPERIMENTAL DESIGNS,\"Box and Draper's work is essential in the development of experimental designs, influencing modern statistical practices\",16\r\n3308,VARIANCE DISPERSION GRAPH,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,The Variance Dispersion Graph (VDG) is used in the context of Response Surface Design to analyze prediction variance,16\r\n3176,SECOND-ORDER RESPONSE SURFACE,EXPERIMENTAL DESIGNS,Experimental Designs are necessary for implementing Second-Order Response Surfaces in practical applications,13\r\n3311,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,DESIGN POINT,Design points are evaluated within the context of Response Surface Design to determine the response of the model,13\r\n3331,RESPONSE SURFACE DESIGN,A-OPTIMALITY CRITERION,The A-optimality criterion is another method used to assess response surface designs,13\r\n1515,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE TABLE,EXPERIMENTER,The analysis of variance table is utilized by the experimenter to interpret the results of the study.,12\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\nOutput:", "role": "user"}], "parameters": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "frequency_penalty": 0.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1, "presence_penalty": 0.0, "temperature": 0.0, "top_p": 1.0, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}}}, "key": "chat_crea